<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Estado da India</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Estado da Índia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Estado da Índia was the Portuguese administration of its possessions in Asia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>, headed by a viceroy or governor based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a> from 1530. It coordinated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a>'s network of fortified trading posts, and its intervention in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horn-of-africa">Horn of Africa</a>, sending troops to help <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, contributed to the defeat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Estado da Índia was founded seven years after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a> reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calicut">Calicut</a> on 20 May 1498. Early Portuguese voyages brought conflict with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamorin">Zamorin</a> of Calicut. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-alvares-cabral">Pedro Álvares Cabral</a> set up a factory there in 1500, residents attacked it and killed more than fifty Portuguese; Cabral seized ten Arab merchant vessels, killing about six hundred of their crew, and ordered Calicut bombarded for a day. On 25 March 1505 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-i-of-portugal">Manuel I of Portugal</a> appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-almeida">Francisco de Almeida</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">Viceroy of India</a>, on the condition that he set up four forts on the southwestern coast: Anjediva, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannur">Cannanore</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kochi">Cochin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kollam">Quilon</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Almeida based himself at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-manuel">Fort Manuel</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kochi">Cochin</a>, which had become a Portuguese protectorate in 1505. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-de-albuquerque">Afonso de Albuquerque</a> became governor in 1509, and in 1510 the Portuguese took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adil-shahi-dynasty">Bijapur Sultanate</a>. Goa became the main anchorage for the armadas, and the capital moved there from Cochin in 1530. In 1526 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iii-of-portugal">John III of Portugal</a> gave Goa the same legal status as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>, and the foral that did so set the city's local laws and privileges in writing for the first time. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-england">Charles II of England</a> in 1661 as part of the dowry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-of-braganza">Catherine of Braganza</a>.</p><p>Until the 18th century the viceroy at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a> had authority over Portuguese possessions from Southern Africa to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>. Mozambique received its own government in 1752, and in 1844 Goa stopped administering Macao, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solor">Solor</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timor">Timor</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> invaded the Konkan settlements in 1683, and the Treaty of Ponda ended the conflict in 1684. Most of the Northern Province was lost after a Maratha invasion of Bassein in 1739. In 1843 the capital moved to Panjim. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-raj">British Raj</a> ended in 1947, Portuguese India had three divisions: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a>, Damaon and Dio. India annexed the rest in December 1961, and Portugal recognised Indian control in a treaty signed on 31 December 1974.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>A governor or viceroy led the Estado, usually for a three-year term. He was the highest military commander and an administrator, responsible for the armed forces, diplomacy, trade, finance and personnel, and he left Portugal with a written set of orders called a regimento.</p><p>Trade was the main purpose of the Estado: it aimed to take over the Europe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> trade, which had passed mainly through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>, and send it round the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope">Cape of Good Hope</a>. Trade ran through royal agents at royal trade posts, or through independent merchants. Because its purpose was to provide protected havens for trade, the Estado was largely urban, and practically every possession was fortified after the massacre at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calicut">Calicut</a> in 1500. It resembled a network of maritime communications as much as a perimeter of fortresses.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Portuguese India” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…conflict also drew the Horn of Africa into the wider rivalry between the Ottoman Empire and the Portuguese <b>Estado da Índia</b>: the Portuguese intervention of 1541, some 400 musketeers under Cristóvão da Gama, and Ottoman military support for Ahmad made it an African theatre of a global struggle…</blockquote><a href="/empire/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Estado da Índia</b> was created under the first viceroy, Francisco de Almeida, in 1505.</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Afonso de Albuquerque captured Goa from the Bijapur Sultanate in 1510, making it the capital of the <b>Estado da India</b>, an empire of forts and ports including Daman, Diu, Bassein, Cochin, Malacca and Hormuz.</blockquote><a href="/state/portuguese-india">Portuguese India</a></li></ul></section>
